WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.380
Good Morning John. People will often say,
down in the comments, that I'm pretty good

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.670
at talking really fast and by now, I kinda
agree - I've had a lot of practice. But John

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.000
there are a few secrets to being able to talk
really well, really fast.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.829
One of them you can see here in this outtakes
video that we did, in which you see the number

00:00:11.829 --> 00:00:15.929
of times that we screw up. But another of
the secrets has to do with cadance, and fricatives,

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:19.670
and posives and stops. This has led me into
a bit of an obsession with tongue twisters

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.599
and that is why I know what a sibilant fricative
is. A fricative is a noise that you make by

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:26.939
putting two parts of your mouth very close
together and blowing air through them. Basically.

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.849
Like 'F' or 'Th' or 'S' or 'Sh'. Fricatives
are one of the most complicated things that

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:35.530
your mouth will ever do. Even if you are thinking
of something dirty, fricatives are more complicated

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:36.530
than that.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:40.260
So when I say "She sells sea shells by the
sea shore", I'm alternating between two very

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.260
similar sibilant fricatives and the difference
is so slight that my brain gets confused about

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.500
which one I should be doing next. Which is
why when I say "the shells she sells are sea

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.789
shore shells", "shells, the shells she shells,
the shell she sells are sea shore, the shells

00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:56.930
she, the shells she sells are sea shore shells"
which I've had much less practice at, I screw

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.519
up so much. Interestingly, the things that
your tongue have to do play a part in tongue

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:04.129
twisters but it is probably more of a mind
twister than a tongue twister. People reading

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:09.000
tongue twisters silently, actually take longer
to read tongue twisters than non-tongue twisting

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.770
sentances. I know its weird!

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.729
Other fricatives are also hard like "f" but
its even harder when you're mixing fricatives

00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:18.030
like "f" and "Sh", like "fisherman". Like
"The fisherman named fisher who was fishing

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.270
for fish in a fisher until a fish with a grin
pulled fisher the fisherman in. Now they're

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.640
fishing for fisher in the fisher". That tongue
twister relies on more than fricatives, it

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.380
also relies on homophones words that are different
but sound exactly the same.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.520
And then we have a thing that I can't find
a name for so I've been calling Pseudophones.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.420
Pseudophones are words that are similar but
not quite the same and if you say them wrong,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.020
everything explodes. For example my absolute
favourite tongue twister "I slit the sheet,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.320
the sheet I slit, and on the slitted sheet
I sit". The pseudophones in this case being

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.490
slit, sheet and sit. What's worse is that
there are several other pseudophones like

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:54.329
seat, sleet and another one and the more frequently
you say those other pseudophones, the more

00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:57.579
frequently you will accidentally jump to them
while you're talking about the "slitted sheet".

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:00.789
The reason why this is my favourite tongue
twister is because people always end up "S***ing

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:03.619
on the slitted sheet". Another important note
about the "slitted sheet" is that its entirely

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:07.820
iambic. Meaning that accented and non accented
words alternate and that almost every accent

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.550
falls on a pseudophone: "I slit the sheet,
the sheet I slit, and on the slitted sheet

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:13.550
I sit".

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.771
Which brings me to what it is I think is the
most important point about tongue twisters.

00:02:15.771 --> 00:02:20.770
The recognised hardest tongue twisters are
things like the "sheiks sixth sick sheep"

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:25.020
but you never hear about the "sheiks sixth
sick sheep". Just having a dense forest of

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.000
sibilant fricatives is, you know, that's hard
but it's not interesting. So what is interesting

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.300
about tongue twisters? Why do we do it? Why
do we have them and why are they prevalent

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.480
in every single language including American
Sign Language. Finger fumblers: they have

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.020
them in sign language too.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.920
You can analyse all day why tongue twisters
are hard but the reason why we do them is

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.020
because they're fun. The thing that our mouth
and our vocal cords and our diaphragm and

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:52.670
our lungs all do together to make words, is
really amazing. And you could easily argue

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.840
that it is what makes us special as a species.
When you start doing tongue twisters you start

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.560
to understand and see how complicated and
beautiful and interesting it all is. Which

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.121
is why I reached out to Nerdfighteria on Twitter
and I asked people to give me tongue twisters

00:03:04.121 --> 00:03:07.170
in their native language because even if you
don't understand what these people are saying,

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:11.140
the things that their mouths are doing are
amazing. So real quick, I just wanted to share

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:20.960
a few of em:
[A Tongue Twister in Palestinian Arabic]

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.280
[A Tongue Twister in Dutch]
[A Tongue Twister in Icelandic]

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:38.849
[A Tongue Twister in Amahric]
[A Tongue Twister in French]

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:45.900
[A Tongue Twister in Cantonese]
[A Tongue Twister in Norwegian]

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.150
You can see, by watching this, that there
is something universal about tongue twisters

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:53.920
and its not just sibilant fricatives. The
universal thing really is humanity and that

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.260
we enjoy having fun. There's some more non-English
tongue twisters in my pants and John if you

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.200
don't have a copy of 'Fox in Socks', I'm sending
my copy for Henry. John, I'll see you on Wednesday.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.200
Thank you for the assistance of Professor
of Linguistics Joel Anderson and also to his

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.260
friend Anne for getting him in touch with
me. And to all of you in the comments who

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.700
are shouting right now that I must be punished
for making a video that is longer than four

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.130
minutes, I must remind you of the Nerdfighter
montage exception and thus I remain punishment

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.780
free. Now I have a final tongue twister for
you: "Betty Botter bought some butter, 'But,'

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:24.220
she said, 'the butter's bitter; If I put it
in my batter, It will make my batter bitter;

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.380
But a bit of better butter, will make my batter
better.' So she bought some better butter,

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.040
Better than the bitter butter, And she put
it in her batter, And her batter was not bitter;

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.810
So 'twas better Betty Botter Bought a bit
of better butter."

